// cffi U3 — the `f64` RESULT on its own (`%t =d call`), with the `d` temp
// carried into `mkflt` and PRINTED.
//
// Sibling: ffid-f64.mid, which crosses the boundary in BOTH directions with one
// strfromd call (a `d` argument and a `w` return).  This one isolates the
// RESULT half: `atof(s)` takes only a `ptr` and returns a `double`, so the one
// thing under test is that a declared `f64` result is emitted as `=d call` (an
// `xmm` result, no extension) and that the temp is classified as a FLOAT word,
// not a word — which is what lets `mkflt` store it with `stored`.
//
// FAILURE SHAPES, both LOUD (measured as mutations of Mid/Qbe/Lower.elm):
//   * `=l call` for the `f64` result -> `stored %t0` with an `l` temp and QBE
//     refuses the program ("invalid type for first operand %t0 in stored");
//   * `isFloatWordExp` answering False -> the same class of usecheck failure,
//     because the float word would be stored through `storel`.
// So this fixture cannot report a wrong NUMBER: a mis-lowered `d` never runs.
// Its golden `2.5` is the runtime's own float printer, i.e. proof the value
// travelled as a float END TO END.
module Ffidr
export Ffidr.run : val -> val

extern atof(s : ptr) -> f64;

defun Ffidr.run(dummy : val) -> val {
  return mkflt(rawcall atof(rawstr("2.50")));
}
